s so if  the progressive ideology and an agenda  have been steadily advancing now for a  hundred years then why don't you see a  similar progression of thought and  conservatism is it possible to have  progress as a conservative it feels as  if sometimes all I have to say about  politics is no stop or too much how  would a citizen like me convey us a  conservative message that is full of  ideas and hope and real progress for  America yeah that's an excellent  question and it's why I think we have to  realize that conservatism has to be  something more than a mere disposition  it has to be more than a kind of set of  cautions or a kind of a tendency to to  uphold the status quo if conservatives  and and you know that's that's a very  broad term but let's say if if those of  us who value the Constitution and want  to see it restored if we're to make any  any headway it seems to me that the  questioner is exactly right we have to  be able to articulate what it is about  the Constitution that's worthwhile why  why is it that government ought to be  limited what are these rights what are  they what are the foundation of these  rights why ought they to be aimed at why  ought they to be pursued and secured  that's that's the sort of positive brand  of conservatism that it seems to me it  has to be understood and embraced and  and the problem I think with with a lot  of elements of conservatism is right now  it's just a kind of disposition it's a  kind of caution if you think about it by  the way who the people who really behave  like conservatives today well they're  the people we would call liberals again  I'm thinking of the the Supreme Court  confirmation hearings spectacle that we  often see who is it who is up there and  in those in those hearings on those  committees calling for an adherence to  precedent cautioning against judicial  activism it's not the you know that the  so-called conservatives that's so-called  right-wingers it's the left  well why is that it's because the  present order is an order it's a it's a  progressive liberal order it's one  that's been established both through  judicial action and legislative action  over the course of the last 80 80 to 100  years that's that's the current American  paradigm that's where we are as a  country and have been for a long time  thanks again really to both both  political parties and so to simply have  a disposition for caution or a  disposition to for the status quo well  the status quo from the point of view of  someone who's who's a constitutionalist  really isn't satisfactory and the status  quo is the problem  so we need to be able to articulate you  know what is it about the Constitution  that makes it worthy as an object of of  our devotion Melissa from Plymouth  Minnesota how is the modern presidency  been shaped by the progressives what  should the executives role be comparing  the founders to that of progressivism  well I teach two courses on that at  Hills at Hillsdale of course on the  presidency and of course on the  progressives and I treat this question  in both of them at great length so I  suppose we can we can extend this for  another hour or so right the session I'm  sure dr. Arnn won't object to that no  quite very briefly you know I have to  say I think it's the case this is a you  know slight generalization but not the  modern presidency is the progressive  presidency  we look to the president today not  simply as the leader of a single branch  of government and don't get me wrong  it's not like the Federalists didn't  want an energetic executive they did  that that was a defect of the Articles  right but they also believe very  strongly in a separation of powers  Woodrow Wilson wrote famously now  eloquently about this in his book  constitutional government which  published in 1908 and he said you know  the problem with this separation of  powers arrangement is  we don't have any leadership in this  country leadership has to be lodged  somewhere and in our current system it's  sort of a three-headed monster and so  Wilson looked for somewhere some entity  out there in our system of government to  kind of rise above the separation of  powers and be able to bring all of the  branches together and move them in the  direction that he believed history was  traveling and initially Wilson thought  this might be Congress as a young man  he'd figured out that was a mistake  quite pretty quickly and looked and  looked to the presidency right so the  presidency for Wilson was something that  wasn't simply about being an executive  it was about really being a leader and  Wilson distinguished between the  constitutional aspects of the presidency  and the political aspects of the  presidency and the constitutional  aspects you got stuff an article to the  actual powers of the presidency that's  not really very important what really  matters is the president's connection to  public opinion his ability to be able to  draw upon his popular mandate he's the  only officer who gets a national vote  other than the vice-president who runs  runs with him on the same ticket and he  needs to translate that mandate that  popular vote into a direction for the  country as a whole and so the presidency  is the way we're going to rise above the  separation of powers well of course  that's exactly how we look at the  president today we look at elections as  mandates we look to our presidents and  again this is something that that has  been forwarded by presidents of both  parties of course we look to our  presidents as popular leaders as  legislative leaders almost as prime  ministers who are going to we want to  know on the campaign trail you know what  what legislation are you going to pass  when you become president this is you  hopefully know by now is not really the  constitutional role the president  although he's given a given a role in  legislation through the veto so it's  it's a question that requires a much a  much longer answer but there is much  about today's presidency that is the  product of of progressivism I talked  about Wilson but  you could say the same about Roosevelt  as well this comes from Doug from Tucson  Arizona and this will be our final  question for this session what did the  progressives think of political parties  again great great question I tried to  point out here that the progressive  movement was not strictly speaking a  partisan movement or the property of any  one political party of course you had  you know TR and the Republicans and  Wilson and the Democrats and you had  strong progressive elements of both  parties and so as progressive ideology  has has moved forward over the course of  the 20th and now 21st century it's it's  a bipartisan thing in terms of what they  thought about political parties there  was a bit of a dispute about that  between the wing of the movement that  was sort of headed by Theodore Roosevelt  on the wing that was headed by Woodrow  Wilson they both had the same objective  however they both thought that parties  as they existed in the 19th century were  an obstacle to progressive aims  they were undemocratic they got in the  way of the people being able to have  their will expressed in governmental  action they were this unnecessary middle  step party bosses were unaccountable the  party system was corrupt right where  they differed was over what the remedy  was Wilson thought that parties could be  reformed and be made the instruments or  tools of progressive leadership so  Wilson said look we have this separation  of power system we got federalism we got  this fragmentation of power all over the  place if we could just make the parties  accountable to the people that's why I  advocated primaries right if we can make  the parties accountable to the people  put the amateurs in charge not the  professional party leaders then they  could be the tools of leadership right a  strong president of a particular party  could use his party ties to move all of  the officials in the different branches  of government in a single direction it's  a way of coordinating sway of overcoming  separation of powers a way of overcoming  federalism so let's reform parties but  let's make them  progressive tools TR Herbert Crowley as  well as progressive intellectual I was  influential on TR thought parties were  incorrigible they the way they were they  could not be reformed they would always  remain obstacles to to progressive aims  and so therefore they had to be done  away with altogether and so the  Progressive Party the the rubric under  which Roosevelt ran in 1912 was a party  to end parties thank you very much
 

